{
  // The routed bench: one physical build serving S1, S2, S3 and the detector leg of S4, with
  // switch changes instead of re-cabling.
  //
  // ============================== READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING IT ==============================
  //
  // The slot and channel numbers below are PLACEHOLDERS. They are decision D-11 and they cannot
  // be read off the bus: the 3499A's SYSTem:CTYPE? returns "GP RELAY 44471" for every one of the
  // 44471A/D, 44476A/B and 44477A, and its own guide says "you must physically check the modules
  // to determine which one is present". So the map has to be built by looking at the mainframe
  // and tracing the cables.
  //
  // `hp8340b route status` lists every leg with its slot, channels and frequency limit, and
  // flags the ones still marked unverified.
  //
  // The switchModel field is what decides whether a leg may carry band 4, so getting it wrong is
  // the one error here that produces a plausible wrong number rather than an obvious failure.
  //
  // WATCH THE BAND-3 EDGE. Band 3 runs 13.5 to 20.0 GHz, so an 18 GHz leg runs out 2 GHz before
  // the top of it — an 18 GHz leg is NOT "everything except band 4". `route status` colours the
  // limit accordingly and `RoutedLeg.Covers` is the thing to ask, not the switch model.
  // ==========================================================================================

  "dcLimitNote": "The 33311B/C are not DC-blocking. Nothing carrying DC goes through an RF leg — the DUT's 0-10 V sweep ramp and the detector's video output use the 44472A VHF legs (SV), not these.",
  "powerRatingNote": "33311B/C average power rating is 1 W. The DUT unleveled at +20 dBm is 100 mW, so there is margin — but check before routing anything amplified.",

  "sr": [
